    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "3 groups of fetal abnormalities may be detected by amniocentesis performed from the 15th week of pregnancy: chromosome anomalies, over 60 inborn errors of metabolism, and neural tube defects. Potential female carriers of or potential affected males suffering from certain X-linked recessive diseases may be determined. A commitment to abortion if an abnormality is confirmed is implied. There are only minimal maternal risks associated with amniocentesis, the main 1 being isoimmunization in Rh-negative females. Fetal trauma by the needle is rare in early pregnancy, but there is a risk of induced abortion judged to be 1-2%. A recent study showed no significant increase in fetal loss among 1040 women undergoing amniocentesis when compared with controls. The technique is generally carried out only on high risk women. If studies confirm that amniocentesis can be carried out without harm to the fetus there will be increasing demand for the service."
    }
